WEBVTT

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:01.238
Welcome!

00:00:01.238 --> 00:00:04.041
In this Java tutorial, you'll read text a line

00:00:04.041 --> 00:00:06.783
at a time with a buffered reader, which holds

00:00:06.783 --> 00:00:09.709
groups of text in memory, and build a dictionary

00:00:09.709 --> 00:00:12.207
that checks whole words and prefixes, the

00:00:12.207 --> 00:00:13.426
beginnings of words.

00:00:14.095 --> 00:00:16.832
Buffered line processing and clear resource

00:00:16.832 --> 00:00:19.696
lifetimes help applications load saved lists,

00:00:19.696 --> 00:00:22.624
reports, and word dictionaries, while separate

00:00:22.624 --> 00:00:25.551
whole-word and prefix queries let a game reuse

00:00:25.551 --> 00:00:28.352
that data without repeating its file-reading

00:00:28.352 --> 00:00:28.670
code.

00:00:29.315 --> 00:00:32.287
Our word-game developer has a dictionary file

00:00:32.287 --> 00:00:35.457
containing cat, cart, a blank line, dog, and cat

00:00:35.457 --> 00:00:35.853
again.

00:00:35.853 --> 00:00:39.023
We'll keep each nonempty line exactly as written

00:00:39.023 --> 00:00:41.665
and retain each distinct word only once.

00:00:42.418 --> 00:00:45.293
The completed load should report three distinct

00:00:45.293 --> 00:00:45.660
words.

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:48.597
A query for the prefix c a should succeed, while

00:00:48.597 --> 00:00:50.126
z z should find no match.

00:00:50.126 --> 00:00:52.818
We'll read until the file ends and close the

00:00:52.818 --> 00:00:55.143
reader before reporting these results.

00:00:55.811 --> 00:00:58.530
Now that we're in the Workspace, let's open

00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:01.186
Main.java and build the dictionary helper.

00:01:04.957 --> 00:01:08.042
We'll begin with the types for file locations,

00:01:08.042 --> 00:01:11.061
UTF-8 text, the buffered reader, and a set of

00:01:11.061 --> 00:01:11.933
unique words.

00:01:11.933 --> 00:01:14.951
IOException gives the handler a specific type

00:01:14.951 --> 00:01:18.103
for failures while opening, reading, or closing

00:01:18.103 --> 00:01:18.707
the file.

00:01:32.833 --> 00:01:35.503
These imports supply the types' short names.

00:01:35.503 --> 00:01:37.991
Files performs the file operations, while

00:01:37.991 --> 00:01:40.115
HashSet will retain distinct words.

00:01:40.115 --> 00:01:42.603
Before loading the file, let's define the

00:01:42.603 --> 00:01:45.274
question that this set will answer: does any

00:01:45.274 --> 00:01:47.883
stored word begin with the supplied prefix?

00:01:57.063 --> 00:01:59.291
WordChecks groups our helper method.

00:01:59.291 --> 00:02:02.075
The method receives the set and the requested

00:02:02.075 --> 00:02:04.735
prefix as two inputs, and returns a Boolean

00:02:04.735 --> 00:02:05.169
result.

00:02:05.169 --> 00:02:07.953
Because the method is static, the application

00:02:07.953 --> 00:02:10.861
will call it through the WordChecks class name.

00:02:10.861 --> 00:02:13.584
We need to search until a word matches or no

00:02:13.584 --> 00:02:14.388
words remain.

00:02:29.845 --> 00:02:32.217
The loop checks each word's beginning.

00:02:32.217 --> 00:02:34.277
A match returns true immediately.

00:02:34.277 --> 00:02:36.837
A failed comparison only moves the search

00:02:36.837 --> 00:02:39.646
onward; false belongs after the loop, once no

00:02:39.646 --> 00:02:40.458
word matched.

00:02:40.458 --> 00:02:42.955
This result does not depend on the set's

00:02:42.955 --> 00:02:43.953
traversal order.

00:02:43.953 --> 00:02:46.388
Complete membership is different: a set

00:02:46.388 --> 00:02:49.260
containing cart can satisfy prefix c a without

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:51.195
containing c a as a whole word.

00:02:51.222 --> 00:02:54.295
With the query helper ready, let's add Main, the

00:02:54.295 --> 00:02:57.175
class containing the application entry point.

00:02:57.175 --> 00:02:59.864
Its main method is where execution starts.

00:02:59.864 --> 00:03:02.744
We'll put our fixture creation and dictionary

00:03:02.744 --> 00:03:05.369
load inside a try block so an I/O failure

00:03:05.369 --> 00:03:07.097
reaches a matching handler.

00:03:14.907 --> 00:03:17.927
The helper is defined above, but it will not run

00:03:17.927 --> 00:03:18.996
until we call it.

00:03:18.996 --> 00:03:21.891
First, the main method needs a controlled file

00:03:21.891 --> 00:03:22.394
to read.

00:03:22.394 --> 00:03:25.351
We'll create a new practice directory and write

00:03:25.351 --> 00:03:28.182
the developer's five lines there using UTF-8.

00:03:49.073 --> 00:03:51.622
The new directory separates this run from

00:03:51.622 --> 00:03:52.493
earlier files.

00:03:52.493 --> 00:03:55.228
Resolving words dot text describes its child

00:03:55.228 --> 00:03:58.088
location; the write actually creates the file.

00:03:58.088 --> 00:04:01.010
Two adjacent newline characters leave the blank

00:04:01.010 --> 00:04:02.627
line between cart and dog.

00:04:02.627 --> 00:04:05.611
The repeated cat remains in the file even though

00:04:05.611 --> 00:04:08.098
the dictionary will retain it only once.

00:04:08.125 --> 00:04:10.721
Now we'll create the empty set and open a

00:04:10.721 --> 00:04:13.570
buffered reader with the same UTF-8 encoding.

00:04:13.570 --> 00:04:16.610
Declaring the reader in try-with-resources gives

00:04:16.610 --> 00:04:17.876
it a clear lifetime.

00:04:17.876 --> 00:04:20.789
The first read must happen before the loop can

00:04:20.789 --> 00:04:22.625
decide whether a line exists.

00:04:57.245 --> 00:05:00.093
The reader may read ahead into its buffer, while

00:05:00.093 --> 00:05:02.586
each readLine call returns one line's text

00:05:02.586 --> 00:05:04.366
without its ending characters.

00:05:04.366 --> 00:05:06.028
The first value here is cat.

00:05:06.028 --> 00:05:08.876
At the end of the file, a later read will return

00:05:08.876 --> 00:05:11.013
null, meaning there is no next line.

00:05:11.013 --> 00:05:13.861
An empty String is a real line value, so it must

00:05:13.861 --> 00:05:14.870
not end the loop.

00:05:14.924 --> 00:05:17.773
We'll separate the decision to keep reading from

00:05:17.773 --> 00:05:20.088
the decision to store the current line.

00:05:20.088 --> 00:05:22.225
The outer condition checks for null.

00:05:22.225 --> 00:05:24.956
Inside it, a length check allows only nonempty

00:05:24.956 --> 00:05:26.024
text into the set.

00:05:38.593 --> 00:05:41.102
Cat and cart pass both checks and become

00:05:41.102 --> 00:05:41.604
members.

00:05:41.604 --> 00:05:44.426
The blank line passes the null test but fails

00:05:44.426 --> 00:05:46.936
the length test, so only its addition is

00:05:46.936 --> 00:05:47.437
skipped.

00:05:47.437 --> 00:05:50.448
We preserve case and spaces rather than silently

00:05:50.448 --> 00:05:51.577
changing the text.

00:05:51.577 --> 00:05:54.525
The remaining step must advance the reader even

00:05:54.525 --> 00:05:55.968
when nothing was added.

00:06:04.817 --> 00:06:07.230
Each body execution reads the next line.

00:06:07.230 --> 00:06:09.945
That lets the loop reach dog and the repeated

00:06:09.945 --> 00:06:11.453
cat after the blank line.

00:06:11.453 --> 00:06:14.288
The set adds dog, but the equal cat adds no new

00:06:14.288 --> 00:06:14.711
member.

00:06:14.711 --> 00:06:17.425
Once a read returns null, the next guard ends

00:06:17.425 --> 00:06:19.839
the loop without another body execution.

00:06:19.839 --> 00:06:22.372
Leaving the resource block then closes the

00:06:22.372 --> 00:06:24.966
reader; the words already stored in the set

00:06:24.966 --> 00:06:25.992
remain available.

00:06:26.043 --> 00:06:28.935
The load is complete, so we'll report the number

00:06:28.935 --> 00:06:31.647
of retained words and call our helper for the

00:06:31.647 --> 00:06:32.430
two prefixes.

00:06:32.430 --> 00:06:34.900
These prints come after the reader closes

00:06:34.900 --> 00:06:35.684
successfully.

00:06:35.684 --> 00:06:38.516
A load failure will therefore skip these normal

00:06:38.516 --> 00:06:40.805
reports rather than look like an empty

00:06:40.805 --> 00:06:41.468
dictionary.

00:07:03.644 --> 00:07:06.792
The size report counts distinct nonempty words,

00:07:06.792 --> 00:07:08.467
not all five input lines.

00:07:08.467 --> 00:07:11.481
Each prefix call receives the same loaded set

00:07:11.481 --> 00:07:13.624
and its own requested beginning.

00:07:13.624 --> 00:07:16.371
Let's finish the handler so a failed file

00:07:16.371 --> 00:07:19.519
operation reports its actual exception message.

00:07:32.619 --> 00:07:35.777
Our complete program creates the file, reads and

00:07:35.777 --> 00:07:38.540
filters every line, closes the reader, and

00:07:38.540 --> 00:07:40.185
queries the retained set.

00:07:40.185 --> 00:07:42.882
Let's click Run and check the developer's

00:07:42.882 --> 00:07:45.119
expected count and prefix results.

00:07:50.698 --> 00:07:53.153
The count is three: cat, cart, and dog.

00:07:53.153 --> 00:07:55.670
The blank line did not stop reading, and

00:07:55.670 --> 00:07:58.251
repeated cat did not increase membership.

00:07:58.251 --> 00:08:01.272
Prefix c a succeeds because a stored word begins

00:08:01.272 --> 00:08:01.775
with it.

00:08:01.775 --> 00:08:04.545
Prefix z z fails after every stored word has

00:08:04.545 --> 00:08:05.552
been considered.

00:08:05.552 --> 00:08:08.195
These reports follow a successful load and

00:08:08.195 --> 00:08:08.573
close.

00:08:08.643 --> 00:08:11.436
Apply those same rules to a different file.

00:08:11.436 --> 00:08:14.359
We'll use map, a blank line, moss, mud, mint,

00:08:14.359 --> 00:08:17.347
and map again, with no newline after the final

00:08:17.347 --> 00:08:17.672
word.

00:08:17.672 --> 00:08:20.725
We'll also change the first query from c a to m

00:08:20.725 --> 00:08:20.855
i.

00:08:20.855 --> 00:08:23.518
The loader and helper will stay the same.

00:09:03.338 --> 00:09:06.214
Before running, predict all three output lines.

00:09:06.214 --> 00:09:08.906
Track what happens after each returned line,

00:09:08.906 --> 00:09:11.782
including the blank line and the final repeated

00:09:11.782 --> 00:09:12.088
word.

00:09:12.088 --> 00:09:14.842
Decide whether the absence of a final newline

00:09:14.842 --> 00:09:16.800
changes what the reader returns.

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:19.370
Pause here and work out the count and both

00:09:19.370 --> 00:09:20.288
prefix results.

00:09:28.795 --> 00:09:31.835
Let's click Run and compare your prediction with

00:09:31.835 --> 00:09:33.545
the actual dictionary load.

00:09:39.490 --> 00:09:40.663
The count is four.

00:09:40.663 --> 00:09:43.790
Map, moss, mud, and mint are distinct; the blank

00:09:43.790 --> 00:09:46.526
line adds nothing and repeated map adds no

00:09:46.526 --> 00:09:46.982
member.

00:09:46.982 --> 00:09:50.045
Final map is still returned without a following

00:09:50.045 --> 00:09:50.566
newline.

00:09:50.566 --> 00:09:52.651
Only the next read returns null.

00:09:52.651 --> 00:09:55.713
Mint supplies the successful m i match, while z

00:09:55.713 --> 00:09:56.690
z matches none.

00:09:56.773 --> 00:09:59.541
You used a buffered reader and a progress step

00:09:59.541 --> 00:10:02.369
to process the whole file, automatic cleanup to

00:10:02.369 --> 00:10:04.956
finish the read, and a set to retain unique

00:10:04.956 --> 00:10:05.678
exact words.

00:10:05.678 --> 00:10:08.386
The helper then answered whether any of those

00:10:08.386 --> 00:10:10.552
words began with a requested prefix.

00:10:10.552 --> 00:10:13.380
These observed results give the game a reusable

00:10:13.380 --> 00:10:16.028
dictionary behavior; the notebook extends it

00:10:16.028 --> 00:10:18.795
into an object with separate complete-word and

00:10:18.795 --> 00:10:19.698
prefix methods.

00:10:19.791 --> 00:10:22.476
For a transfer question, suppose a dictionary

00:10:22.476 --> 00:10:23.491
stores only book.

00:10:23.491 --> 00:10:26.236
What should a whole-word query for b o report,

00:10:26.236 --> 00:10:29.100
and what should a prefix query for the same text

00:10:29.100 --> 00:10:29.517
report?

00:10:29.517 --> 00:10:32.262
Explain which operation each query needs, then

00:10:32.262 --> 00:10:34.530
use that distinction as you build your

00:10:34.530 --> 00:10:36.141
dictionary in the notebook.
